\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Game Theory: A Summary of Academic Papers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 8, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At its core, economics is the study of how economic agents behave, interact,
 and make decisions in the presence of scarce resources.
 Game theory is a methodology used to study strategic decision making and
 the interactions between agents in strategic situations.
 The payoffs of agents in noncooperative game theory depend not only on
 their own actions, but also on the actions of other agents, which means
 the beliefs of players are endogendous.
 Remember, in a Nash equilibrim, each player formulates optimal strategies
 assuming that the other players will also be utilizing optimal strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the techniques of game theory can be applied in a wide variety of contexts,
 and they have become pervasive in economic research.
 However, these devices often conflict in determining reasonable equilibria
 for economic games, and they offer differerent positive explanations concerning
 observed behavior.
 Thus, game theory offers a wide variety of opportunities for experimental
 research, especially for the purpose of evaluating predictions against
 alternative theories.
 In this summary, I cover the problems associated with asymmetric information,
 a popular topic of game theory, within the context of industrial organization.
 I also provide an expository on some conerning 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signaling,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is often a solution to the problems cause by asymmetric information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Industrial organization has always been a popular field to conduct experimental
 research.
 As experimental economics has progressed, so has the complexity of the
 markets studied in the lab.
 Some of the areas of interest include: product quality, asymmetric information,
 and market failures.
 Moral hazard is a major problem that arises due to the presence of asymmetric
 information within a market.
 For clarification, moral hazard is simply identified as a situation in
 which one agent decides on how much risk to take, while another agents
 bears some, or even all, of the negative consequences of those risky choices.
 In the context of industrial organization, moral hazard occurs when both
 sellers' costs and buyers' values depend on product quality.
 The sellers know the quality of their products, while the quality cannot
 be directly observed by buyers.
 One possible solution to the problems of moral hazard and asymmetric informatio
n involves simple, indirect communication between agents, also known as
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signals.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Signaling Game: Miller and Plott (1985)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Miller and Plott (1985) designed and conducted an experiment in which sellers
 were allowed to invest in costly, observable signals.
 To elaborate, sellers possessed products that were exogenously designated
 as either grade 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

regular
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or grade 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

super.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Buyers had higher value for products graded as super, but the grade could
 not be observed prior to purchase.
 Sellers could add units of quality, which were also valued by buyers, and
 these added quality units could be observed prior to purchase.
 The cost of adding quality to supers was lower than the cost of adding
 quality to regulars.
 Due to these conditions, the possibility of signaling existed in all of
 the constructed eleven markets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In several experiments, subjects had experience since they participated
 in earlier series, while the same set of subjects were used in experiments
 11 and 12.
 Markets proceeded in a sequence of periods, and in each period, sellers
 had only two units of the same grade to sell.
 For different periods, sellers were randomly assigned the type of grades
 they would be selling with half the sellers designated as supers and half
 as regulars.
 Sellers could add quality to a unit at a cost which depended upon the grade
 of the unit.
 In all experiments, the sellers of regular grades faced a constant marginal
 cost of $.15 to add quality.
 For supers, the marginal cost of adding quality was $.07 in only three experimen
ts and $.02 in the remaining ones.
 All buyers had the same redemption schedules, which depended on both the
 grade and quality of the unit purchased.
 Important to note, the difference in value for buyers between supers and
 regulars was held constant at $2.00.
 Also, buyers received a bonus if at least one unit was purchased.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Except for changing the grades of sellers, the trading periods for a given
 market were identical.
 Bids and offers were announced verbally and written on a chalkboard, and
 they remained until accepted or cancelled.
 Acceptances could be made at any time, but sellers were required to cancel
 offers when they had no more units.
 Information about agreed upon contracts was recorded on a second contract
 and carried on for as many periods as possible.
 Thus, subjects always had access to information from previous periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Models to be Evaluated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Several different models and their respective predictions were examined
 using this experiment.
 A brief description of each model is provided below.
 Note that all models predicted the quality of regulars would be the same,
 and that the quality of all supers would be the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
The Full Information Model
\emph default
: Markets behave as if buyers are fully informed about which sellers have
 regulars and which have supers.
 This could arise becase sellers want to establish a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reputation of honesty
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 due to the game being repeated over multiple periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
The Naive Model
\emph default
: Buyers may never realize that the quality of the unit conveys information
 about its underlying type.
 Consequently, they would treat the purchase of a good as a lottery between
 the value of a regular and the value of a super at a given level of quantity.
 The subjective probabilities are determined by rational expectations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
The Pure Pooling Model
\emph default
: Sellers of regulars and supers purchase an identical level of quality
 because they believe buyers will accept nothing other than this particular
 quality.
 Thus, buyers are unable to distinguish between the two grades because the
 prices are also identical, so they treat a purchase as a lottery.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Partial Pooling Model
\emph default
: Only regular units trade at a low quality, while both grades are traded
 at a higher quality in a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of grades.
 The two qualities trade at different prices.
 The holders of regulars are indifferent between selling at the two grades,
 while the holders of supers may sell all units at the higher quality.
 Prices should increase to the demand price, which reflects uncertainty.
 Probabilities are updated based on rational expectations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Most Efficient Signaling Equilibrium and Rothschild-Stiglitz
\emph default
: Buyers are aware of the relationship between quality and grade, so when
 they observe the quality, they know the grade for certain.
 Sellers have no incentive to change the quality they offer given the grade.
 The most efficient signaling equilibrium is the list of prices and qualities
 that maximizes total surplus.
 Rothschild and Stiglistz, on the other hand, suggest that certain conditions
 are necessary for an equilibrium to be achieved.
 Sellers may prefer a pooling equilibrium to a most efficient signaling
 equilibrium and thereby have an incentive to pool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Inefficient Signaling Equilibria
\emph default
: The inefficient signaling equilibria are solutions to the necessary conditions
 of the most efficient signaling equilibrium with the efficient solution
 removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to evaluate the results of the experiments, time series statistics
 of all periods were implemented, and three primary aspects were considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Excess value
\emph default
: The amount paid over the unit's value in case the unit is a regular.
 So, excess value is a reflection of the buyer's confidence that the unit
 is a super.
 It is the maximum possible loss the buyer can face by purchasing a unit
 of certain quality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Quality of the unit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Opportunity cost
\emph default
: The opportunity cost that holders of a certain grade experienced because
 they did not sell their units in the price and quality range in which the
 other grade was trading.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The major conclusion from the paper is that signaling is a real phenomenon.
 When the marginal cost of signaling was relatively low, quality separation
 occured, and there was no evidence of pooling.
 However, it should be noted that two markets with low marginal costs of
 signaling failed to produce signaling.
 This result implies that quality separation of grades is not a sufficient
 condition for the presence of signaling.
 Next, three of the six markets that produced signaling are statistically
 within $.05 of the predicted equilibria in the last two periods.
 While the other three markets that produced signaling did not reach the
 equilibrium, two of the markets were still converging to some signaling
 equilibrium.
 Now, for three of the signaling markets, the perfect information model
 and the efficient signaling equilibrium were rejected in favor of the inefficie
nt equilibrium model.
 Yet, it was conjectured that two of the experiments were converging to
 the most efficient signaling equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moving on, when the marginal cost of signaling was relatively high, quality
 separation occured infrequently and aspects of pooling were observed.
 One experiment was an excellent candidate for the pooling equilibrium model.
 The pooling equilibrium model was rejected for two experiments, but they
 both had elements of the partial pooling equilibrium behavior.
 Consequently, it was unclear to the authors whether the pooling behavior
 involved the type of cycling suggested by the Rothschild-Stiglitz equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, the authors provided several conjectures concerning the dynamics
 of the signaling equilibrium models based on the observed data.
 It should be noted that quality is not the only possible signal within
 a given market.
 Note that any market which enables face-to-face interaction allows for
 the possibility of reputation development among sellers.
 So, the application of signaling equilibrium models is dependent upon the
 market because the institutional environment affects the nature and identificat
ion of the signal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Bargaining Game: Radner and Schotter (1989)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Researchers conducting bargaining experiments often focus on the relative
 roles played by strategic considerations implied by game-theoretic models.
 A main concern among empirical investigators of barganing is the nonnegligible
 frequency of disagreements and costly delays, even when mutually profitable
 agreements are available.
 The game-theoretic models used to explain these disagreements and delays
 impose the feature of incomplete information.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In these modles, bargainers are uncertain about some important features
 of one anothers' situations, which, if known, would influence the distribution
 of profits between the parties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (Kagel and Roth, 1995).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
The Bargaining Mechansim
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Radner and Schotter (1989) presented results from eight different experiments
 that were constructed to test the theoretical and empirical properties
 of the sealed-bid bargaining mechanism.
 To elaborate, consider a situation in which a potential buyer and seller
 are bargaining over the terms of a possible trade of a single object.
 The buyer holds a private valuation for the object, and the seller incurs
 a private cost only if the object is traded.
 The sealed-bid mechanism works as follows: the buyer and seller simultaneously
 submit individual bids.
 If the buyer's bid is greater than or equal to the seller's bid, then trade
 takes place, and the buyer pays the seller a price equal to the average
 of the two bids.
 If the buyer's bid is less than the seller's bid, then no trade takes place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The buyer only observes his value, so his bidding strategy is a function
 that determines his bid for each possible value that he may have.
 The seller only observes his cost, so his bidding strategy is a function
 that determines his bid for each possible cost that he may incur.
 If a trades takes place, then the buyer's profit is his value for the good
 minus its price, and the seller's profit is the price of the good minus
 his cost incurred from trading.
 In the model, it is assumed that both parties are risk-neutral, expected
 utility (profit) maximizers.
 Thus, the characteristics just described determine a noncooperative game.
 An equilibrium of the game is a pair of strategies such that neither player
 can increase his expected utility (profit) by unilaterally changing his
 strategy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, in a previous paper, it was shown that if the buyer's value and the
 seller's cost are uniformly distributed over the closed interval [0,100],
 then there exists a pair of linear bidding strategies that together form
 an equilibrium of the sealed-bid mechanism game.
 In fact, this equilibrium has very strong welfare properties.
 However, the sealed-bid mechansim has an abundance of equilibria, some
 of which may be entirely worthless in terms of efficiency.
 On the whole, the authors hoped to answer several questions concerning
 the mechansim and its strategies using the results of their experiments:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Did buyers and sellers employ linear strategies? If so, did they use the
 ones predicted by the equilibrium?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
How well did the mechanism perform and what accounts for this?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Does the bidding behavior of subjects change as the number of rounds in
 the experiment increases?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Each buyer and seller drew 15 envelopes, which contained a number inside,
 from a pile of 500.
 The numbers were randomly generated from a commonly known probability distribut
ion.
 After observing and recording the realization in the envelope, subjects
 wrote their bids on pieces of paper, which were then collected.
 These bids were then randomly sorted into pairs, each containing the bid
 of one buyer and one seller.
 Then, the bids were compared, and prices and payoffs were determined according
 to the rules of the sealed-bid mechanism.
 In almost all experiments, only information about the price and trade results
 of a subject's own transaction were revealed.
 In subsequent rounds, subjects were paired against the same member.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors ran eight separate experiments with eight different groups of
 about 20 subjects.
 Below is a descriptive list that provides the unique detail associated
 with each experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 1
\emph default
: This was a baseline that employed a uniform prior distribution for both
 buyers and sellers.
 Its linear equilibrium is identical to the equilibrium mentioned in the
 previous section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 2
\emph default
: Identical to experiment 1 except price information concerning all pairs
 was written on a blackboard after each round.
 This was done to foster learning without increasing the number of rounds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 3
\emph default
: This experiment had a different set of price and trade rules compared
 to the previous two.
 The rules were changed so that a buyer's bid needed to exceed a seller's
 bid by 25 or more in order for a trade to occur.
 If a trade was made, the price was 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{(v+c+50)}{3}$
\end_inset

.
 This experiment was constructed to test the Revelation Principle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 4
\emph default
: This experiment is equivalent to a mechanism in which the buyer makes
 a take-it-or-leave-it bid to the seller, who merely accepts it or rejects
 it.
 This experiment was employed to test the robustness of the sealed-bid mechanism
 to changes in its price-formation rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 5
\emph default
: This experiment ran the baseline experiment with nonuniform but symmetric
 prior distributions.
 An equilibrium with linear strategies also existed for these prior distribution
s.
 The authors ran this experiment in an attempt to increase the probability
 of trade, and hence the experience of subjects, without increasing the
 number of rounds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 6
\emph default
: This experiment is identical to experiment 5 except that the number of
 rounds was increased to 40 instead of 15.
 The authors wished to test if the behavior of subjects changed as the number
 of rounds increased.
 They also wanted to see if learning could increase the goodness of fit
 between the observed and predicted behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 7
\emph default
: Face-to-face bargaining was used as opposed to the sealed-bid mechanism.
 This experiment wished to compare the results of the sealed-bid mechanism
 with another commonly used bargaining alternative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Experiment 8
\emph default
: Subjects played for points, which were transformed into probabilities
 of winning a fixed lottery prize, in order to induce risk-neutrality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the authors were concered about a direct test of the sealed-bid mechansim,
 they primarily focused on the results of Experiments 1, 2, and 5, while
 the results of Experiments 6 and 7 were analyzed separately.
 Experiments 3, 4, and 8 confound the data since they introduced different
 price formations and/or trading rules.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, it appears that the bidding strategies employed by both buyers
 and sellers were close to linear when the underlying probability distributions
 in the experiment were uniform.
 Figures representing the bid-value relationship lend credence to this conjectur
e.
 The authors performed more rigorous tests for linearity by running various
 regressions on the possible structure of the bidding functions for each
 buyer and seller in all experiments but one.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
v=\alpha+\beta_{1}V+\beta_{2}V^{2}+\beta_{3}V^{3}+u_{b}\\
c=\alpha+\beta_{1}C+\beta_{2}C^{2}+\beta_{3}C^{3}+u_{s}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

They ran an F-test on the results to test the null hypothesis that the coefficie
nts of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{3}$
\end_inset

 were both zero in each regression.
 None of the regressions demonstrated a significant difference from zero
 at the 99% confidence level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, when the probability distributions were not uniform, the results
 suggested that step-function equilibria began to emerge.
 This conjecture was reinforced when the pattern was even more pronounced
 as the number of rounds increased from 15 to 40.
 Thus, it seems as though the underlying prior distribution influences the
 type of equilibrium that may arise from a sealed-bid mechansim.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, tests were constructed in order to determine if subjects behaved according
 to the predictions of the theory and employed the equilibrium bid functions.
 The data of buyers and sellers were pooled in each experiment, and pooled
 regressions were run on the observations below and above the critical kink
 in the equilibrium bid function.
 The results support the hypothesis that bidding behavior for both buyers
 and sellers is different on either side of the kink point.
 Moreover, sellers bid with a 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 that was not statistically different from the predicted coefficient.
 Buyers, on the other hand, did not shave their bids by the amount predicted,
 so their bids were too high.
 Finally, subjects bid their respective value or cost when the linear equilibriu
m predicts they should.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The performance of the sealed-bid mechanism was another important topic
 of focus.
 In later rounds of the experiments, subjects increased the number of successful
 trades.
 In fact, the number of successful trades was close to the figure predicted
 by the linear equilibrium model.
 Furthermore, subjects were able to capture over 90% of the gains from trade
 expected from the linear equilibrium, which only achieves second-best gains,
 and over 85% of the gains in terms of the first-best gains from trade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Cournot Game: Rassenti, Reynolds, Smith, and Szidarovszky (2000)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Cournot competition is a popular economic model that is primarily applied
 in the field of industrial organization.
 The Cournot game is used to describe an industry structure in which sellers
 compete on the amount of output they will produce.
 Companies select output quantities simultaneously and independently of
 each other, which, in turn, determines the market price of all units sold.
 The Cournot model provides an intriguing set of equilibrium and convergence
 predictions.
 While there is a unique pure strategy Nash equilibrium for the static Cournot
 game, behavior may not converge to this Nash equilibrium with repeated
 play.
 Convergence depends on the learning or adjustment process utilized by subjects.
 Rassenti, Reynolds, Smith, and Szidarovszky (2000) designed a series of
 experiments in order to analyze the convergance to equilibrium behavior
 under the backdrop of the Cournot oligopoly model of output choice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The experimental design offered the opportunity to distinguish between competing
 theories of learning or adjustment.
 A fixed group of five subjects, each taking on the role of a seller, participat
ed in an experiment that was ran for a total of 75 trading periods.
 At the beginning of each trading period, each subject simultaneously chose
 a quantity of output to sell, which was inputted to a computer.
 The market price was determined by an inverse demand function, which was
 public information.
 In a new experiment, each subject was assigned a different marginal cost
 of output that was designated as private information, so no information
 was provided about the marginal costs of other subjects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A subject's payoff at the end of a trading period was equal to the output
 chosen by the subject times the difference between the market price and
 his marginal cost.
 Note that payoff conditions remained constant during the repeated trials
 within an experiment.
 Also, while subjects were given an equal amount of starting capital, going
 bankrupt was a legitimate outcome for sellers since negative payoffs in
 a trading period were possible.
 Due to the experimental conditions just described, the private payoffs
 were asymmetric, so there were no symmetric Nash equilibria.
 Above all, the private payoff condition prevented subjects from computing
 noncooperative Nash equilibrium and joint profit maximizing outputs and
 playing the corresponding strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At the end of a trading period, subjects observed the market price of output,
 the output of their rivals, and their own payoff.
 The computer screen listed up to eight periods of market outcomes.
 The amount of information provided about the choices of rivals was a treatment
 variable.
 In one group of experiments (type 1), subjects observed the choices of
 each one of their rivals, while in another group (type 2), subjects only
 observed the total output chosen by their rivals.
 Remember, the choices of a subject's rivals entered into his payoff function
 only through the total output chosen by the rivals.
 So, most of the learning and decision adjusment processes proposed for
 this Cournot oligopoly environment depended only on aggregate output of
 rivals in previous periods.
 Yet, the provision of information about individual choices of rivals may
 have influenced the learning process.
 Thus, the authors hypothesized that the experiments which contributed the
 additional information would yield higher outputs than the other experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Possible Adjustment Processes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As subjects gained experience making decisions and observed the strategies
 of rival subjects, it was conjectured that they may learn more about the
 market environment and adjust their behavior accordingly.
 A number of decision adaptations processes have been proposed in the literature
 on learning and convergence in games.
 The authors focused on several of these processes in the context of the
 Cournot experiment enviornment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Best response dynamic
\emph default
: Each subject sets his current output equal to the best (i.e., current period
 payoff maximizing) response to the last period outputs of his rivals.
 However, this process does not yield convergence to the Nash equilibrium
 for the experimental environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Partial adjustment to best response
\emph default
: A simple linear specification of this process would be a weighted average
 of a subject's last period output and the subject's best response to rival's
 last period output.
 Convergence to the Nash equilibrium arises if subjects have a common value
 for the adjustment parameter and this parameter is between 1/3 and 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Fictitious play
\emph default
: It is assumed that each subject chooses current output to maximize their
 expected payoff, where the choices of rivals are assumed to be drawn from
 the empirical frequency distribution of past their past choices.
 THis is equivalent to having each player choose output in the current period
 as a best response to historical average outputs of rival players.
 This process triggers a stable output path that converges to the static
 Nash equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Adaptive learning
\emph default
: The player eventually chooses only strategies that can be justified in
 terms of the past play of his rivals.
 The strategy choices of a player are eventually in a set of undominated
 strategies, given some restriction on the set of rivals' strategies.
 If all players adopt the adaptive learning sequence and the sequence converges,
 then the convergent point must be a Nash equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Experimental Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The authors ran five experiments for each of the three different experiment
 types.
 Two of the experiment types altered the amount of information given to
 subjects concerning rivals' choices, and they were already touched upon
 in a prior subsection.
 The third type of experiment consisted of 15 rounds in which a different
 set of marginal cost and exchange rate parameters were used instead of
 the usual parameters employed by other trading periods.
 Also, subjects were able to observe the past choices of individual rivals
 in this type of experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Time series plots for total output reveal that there is considerable intertempor
al variation in total output within each experiment, which ocassionally
 persists for the duration of each experiment.
 Yet, the variability in total output does decrease over time.
 Total outputs, taken as an average across experiments for each type of
 experiment, are slightly higher than the Nash equilibrium prediction.
 This result leads to the conclusion that treatment conditions did not have
 a significant effect on total output levels.
 For most experiments, the actual movement of total ouput over time appears
 to be inconsistent with both the best response dynamic and fictitious play.
 Finally, there is no evidence of tacit collusion due to repeated plays
 of the game.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to the Nash equlibrium prediction, marginal costs and output are
 inversely related.
 This means that output should fall as marginal costs increase.
 However, average output per seller fails to conform to this pattern.
 So, observed behavior for individual subject sellers does not converge
 to the static Nash equilibrium predictions for individual output choices.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to obtain more information about individual behavior, the authors
 formulate and estimated a possible linear decision rule utilized by each
 subject.
 This linear decision rule is assumed to be determined by lagged and twiced
 lagged sum of rival's outputs and lagged own output choice.
 Since individual output was nonstationary for many subjects, first-differences
 were taken in order to estimate the decision rule.
 After four separate and distinct tests of hypotheses, estimation results
 signify the failure of individual outputs to converge to Nash equilibria
 outputs, but the source of the failure is unknown.
 In fact, one-third of subjects fail to respond to changes in rivals' outputs.
 Any convergent adjustment process requires subjects to take their rivals'
 actions into account when choosing their own output.
 However, there is a possibility that the authors fail to detect a response
 to rivals' output changes because the decision rule they estimate may be
 misspecified.
 Some subjects may use a nonlinear decision rule and/or different lag structures
 for rivals' output.
\end_layout

